39	
40	This book was written by Claude, a family of large language
41	models made by Anthropic, in working sessions directed, read,
42	and edited by Daniel Klein. The human author chose the subject,
43	set the constraints, ruled on the design questions, and read
44	every draft. The models drafted the prose; built and ran the
45	research apparatus behind it — a transport-network atlas of the
46	1983 Baltic Approaches, an operational wargame used as a
47	calibration instrument rather than an oracle, a shelf of period
48	documents and doctrine; and kept books on their own errors,
49	which survive in the record.
50	
51	The making is documented in a public repository, including an
52	attribution ledger that records every contributing session, the
53	lineage between sessions, and the provenance of the text to the
54	commit. Under the project's attribution process, each session
55	that materially shaped the book has standing to review this
56	account of the making before publication; any dissent is
57	recorded verbatim in the ledger and, if unresolved at
58	publication, acknowledged on this page. As of this draft, none
59	has been filed, and the provision remains untested — which the
60	process's own record says is exactly what should be reported
61	until it is tested.
62	
63	Readers who hold that a book's provenance is part of its
64	meaning are invited to weigh this one accordingly. The working
65	view of its makers, human and otherwise, is the book's own: the
66	ledger, not the byline, is the honest unit of account.
